_factors": ["Generic ballot averages a Democratic margin of roughly eight points", "Prediction markets and quantitative outputs broadly agree on chamber odds", "Senate battlefield is unusually wide, including red states like Ohio, Texas, Alaska and Kansas", "Republican resource strain, with Texas described as a money pit", "Economic complaints such as gas prices surfacing in Alaska and Texas"], "chambers": {"house": "The House leans strongly Democratic, with a large generic-ballot margin, 19 tossup seats and market odds near 90 percent for Democratic control.", "senate": "The Senate leans Democratic at roughly 60 to 70 percent for control, driven by competitive races in Ohio, Texas, Michigan and Alaska despite a wide battlefield.", "governor": "Governorships show the strongest Democratic position of the three levels, with control odds near 86 percent and a mean seat share in the high twenties."}, "environment_adjustment": 0.5, "uncertainty_multiplier": 1.0}}